1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,679
A phone runs on a lithium cell.

2
00:00:02,679 --> 00:00:05,038
Many pressure cookers are aluminium.

3
00:00:05,038 --> 00:00:07,407
Tap water is cleaned with chlorine.

4
00:00:07,807 --> 00:00:09,885
Lithium, aluminium, chlorine.

5
00:00:09,885 --> 00:00:13,742
Each turns up in a famous
exception to the periodic trends.

6
00:00:14,192 --> 00:00:15,463
Diya wonders why.

7
00:00:15,463 --> 00:00:19,754
If the trends are so neat, why
does her textbook list exceptions?

8
00:00:20,104 --> 00:00:25,139
Every exception has a reason: size,
shells, and how electrons are arranged.

9
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:30,100
By the end, you will explain the
famous exceptions, and see why lithium

10
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:31,639
behaves like magnesium.

11
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:33,869
So what is an exception?

12
00:00:34,219 --> 00:00:38,417
The trend says ionization
enthalpy rises across a period.

13
00:00:38,417 --> 00:00:39,893
Watch period two.

14
00:00:40,243 --> 00:00:42,403
Lithium, five hundred and twenty.

15
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:44,994
Beryllium, eight hundred and ninety nine.

16
00:00:44,994 --> 00:00:47,651
Then boron drops to eight hundred and one.

17
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:50,428
Carbon, one thousand and eighty six.

18
00:00:50,428 --> 00:00:53,299
Nitrogen, one thousand
four hundred and two.

19
00:00:53,299 --> 00:00:57,069
Then oxygen drops to one thousand
three hundred and fourteen.

20
00:00:57,419 --> 00:00:59,429
Fluorine and neon climb again.

21
00:00:59,429 --> 00:01:01,234
Two dips, two exceptions.

22
00:01:01,234 --> 00:01:03,098
All in kilojoules per mole.

23
00:01:03,748 --> 00:01:06,515
Let's open them up on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:06,515 --> 00:01:08,482
First, beryllium and boron.

25
00:01:08,832 --> 00:01:11,411
Beryllium loses a two s electron.

26
00:01:11,411 --> 00:01:14,343
Boron loses its single two p electron.

27
00:01:14,743 --> 00:01:19,410
An s electron gets closer to the
nucleus than a p electron of the same

28
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:20,310
shell.

29
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:21,497
It penetrates more.

30
00:01:21,897 --> 00:01:26,755
So boron's two p electron is better
shielded by the inner electrons, and

31
00:01:26,755 --> 00:01:29,814
easier to remove, even with more protons.

32
00:01:30,464 --> 00:01:32,425
Now nitrogen and oxygen.

33
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:37,845
Nitrogen's three two p electrons sit
in separate orbitals, by Hund's rule.

34
00:01:38,245 --> 00:01:41,729
Oxygen has four, so two of
them must share one orbital.

35
00:01:42,129 --> 00:01:45,916
Those two repel each other,
so one is easier to remove.

36
00:01:45,916 --> 00:01:48,554
That is why oxygen dips below nitrogen.

37
00:01:49,204 --> 00:01:50,483
Side by side.

38
00:01:50,483 --> 00:01:53,218
Boron loses a shielded p electron.

39
00:01:53,218 --> 00:01:57,172
Oxygen loses an electron
pushed out by its partner.

40
00:01:57,572 --> 00:01:58,918
Pause and think.

41
00:01:58,918 --> 00:02:03,624
Which has the higher first ionization
enthalpy, nitrogen or oxygen?

42
00:02:06,824 --> 00:02:07,724
Nitrogen!

43
00:02:07,596 --> 00:02:12,154
None of its two p electrons are
paired, so there is no extra repulsion

44
00:02:12,154 --> 00:02:13,615
to help remove one.

45
00:02:14,265 --> 00:02:16,126
Now electron gain enthalpy.

46
00:02:16,126 --> 00:02:18,978
Here the exception sits
in group seventeen.

47
00:02:19,328 --> 00:02:20,228
Step one.

48
00:02:20,166 --> 00:02:24,626
Fluorine releases three hundred and
twenty eight kilojoules per mole when

49
00:02:24,626 --> 00:02:26,201
it gains an electron.

50
00:02:26,201 --> 00:02:29,290
Chlorine releases three
hundred and forty nine.

51
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:30,385
Chlorine wins.

52
00:02:31,135 --> 00:02:32,035
Step two.

53
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:33,294
Fluorine is tiny.

54
00:02:33,294 --> 00:02:38,353
The new electron enters a crowded second
shell, and suffers strong repulsion.

55
00:02:39,103 --> 00:02:40,003
Step three.

56
00:02:39,956 --> 00:02:44,346
In chlorine, it enters the third
shell, with more room, so the repulsion

57
00:02:44,346 --> 00:02:45,349
is much less.

58
00:02:46,099 --> 00:02:47,005
Step four.

59
00:02:47,005 --> 00:02:48,934
The same in group sixteen.

60
00:02:48,934 --> 00:02:53,208
Oxygen releases one hundred and
forty one, sulphur two hundred.

61
00:02:53,958 --> 00:02:55,029
Remember this.

62
00:02:55,029 --> 00:02:59,596
Every exception has a reason: shielding,
paired electrons, or a crowded

63
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:00,567
small shell.

64
00:03:01,267 --> 00:03:04,206
Now, one worked example
on the second period.

65
00:03:04,506 --> 00:03:06,750
Boron forms BF four minus.

66
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:09,934
Aluminium forms A l F six, three minus.

67
00:03:09,934 --> 00:03:12,293
Why can boron hold only four?

68
00:03:12,793 --> 00:03:14,690
Boron is in period two.

69
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:18,954
Its valence shell has one two
s and three two p orbitals.

70
00:03:18,954 --> 00:03:20,149
Four orbitals.

71
00:03:20,899 --> 00:03:24,277
Four orbitals hold at
most four bonding pairs.

72
00:03:24,277 --> 00:03:26,964
So boron's maximum covalency is four.

73
00:03:27,714 --> 00:03:32,489
Aluminium is in period three, with
one three s, three three p, and five

74
00:03:32,489 --> 00:03:33,779
three d orbitals.

75
00:03:33,779 --> 00:03:36,791
Nine in all, so it can hold six fluorines.

76
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:38,922
Check the charges.

77
00:03:38,922 --> 00:03:43,667
Boron plus three, with four fluorides
at minus one, gives minus one.

78
00:03:43,667 --> 00:03:47,498
Aluminium plus three with six
fluorides gives minus three.

79
00:03:48,148 --> 00:03:50,917
The first member of
each group is different.

80
00:03:50,917 --> 00:03:54,971
Lithium and beryllium form compounds
with pronounced covalent character,

81
00:03:54,971 --> 00:03:57,059
unlike the rest of their groups.

82
00:03:57,459 --> 00:04:02,342
Lithium behaves more like magnesium,
and beryllium more like aluminium.

83
00:04:02,342 --> 00:04:04,783
This is the diagonal relationship.

84
00:04:05,183 --> 00:04:11,494
The reasons are small size, a large charge
to radius ratio, and high electronegativity.

85
00:04:11,894 --> 00:04:16,960
And second period atoms form strong
multiple bonds, such as C double bond

86
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:20,333
C, N triple bond N, and C double bond O.

87
00:04:20,983 --> 00:04:22,448
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:04:22,798 --> 00:04:27,802
Students reason that boron has more
protons than beryllium, so its ionization

89
00:04:27,802 --> 00:04:29,594
enthalpy must be higher.

90
00:04:29,994 --> 00:04:34,369
But beryllium is eight hundred and
ninety nine, boron only eight hundred

91
00:04:34,369 --> 00:04:35,269
and one.

92
00:04:35,123 --> 00:04:37,130
Boron's p electron is shielded.

93
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,489
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:39,489 --> 00:04:44,913
NEET has asked the ionization enthalpy
order of boron to oxygen, the halogen

95
00:04:44,913 --> 00:04:49,787
with the most negative electron
gain enthalpy, and diagonal pairs.

96
00:04:50,387 --> 00:04:51,610
Exam level now.

97
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:53,518
Which statement is correct?

98
00:04:53,518 --> 00:04:54,793
Pause and solve.

99
00:05:00,193 --> 00:05:01,606
The answer is C.

100
00:05:01,606 --> 00:05:05,757
Beryllium has a higher first
ionization enthalpy than boron.

101
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:09,411
Eight hundred and ninety nine
beats eight hundred and one.

102
00:05:09,911 --> 00:05:12,161
A is wrong: nitrogen is higher.

103
00:05:12,161 --> 00:05:14,818
B is wrong: chlorine is more negative.

104
00:05:15,318 --> 00:05:16,379
D is wrong.

105
00:05:16,379 --> 00:05:20,632
Lithium's compounds are covalent
in character, like magnesium's.

106
00:05:21,233 --> 00:05:22,693
Here is your recap.

107
00:05:22,693 --> 00:05:24,194
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:27,394 --> 00:05:30,231
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:30,631 --> 00:05:31,531
One.

110
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:35,457
Why is boron's ionization
enthalpy lower than beryllium's?

111
00:05:36,257 --> 00:05:37,157
Two.

112
00:05:36,806 --> 00:05:41,943
Why does chlorine have a more negative
electron gain enthalpy than fluorine?

113
00:05:42,743 --> 00:05:43,643
Three.

114
00:05:43,344 --> 00:05:46,891
Name the two diagonal pairs,
and one reason for them.

115
00:05:47,791 --> 00:05:53,080
Next time: why do atoms bond at all, and
what decides the shape of a molecule?

116
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:56,656
We find out in chemical bonding
and molecular structure.
